<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byzantine–Seljuk wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1048">1048</a>–<a href="/years/1308">1308</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Byzantine–Seljuk wars were the conflicts between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, from Seljuk raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in the mid-11th century to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>. The Byzantine defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071 opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to Turkish settlement, and Byzantine appeals for help led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. The Komnenian emperors recovered western Anatolia, but the defeat at Myriokephalon in 1176 ended hopes of retaking the interior.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzantine-seljuk-wars.jpg" alt="Byzantine–Seljuk wars" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Tourskin; derivative work by Stegop · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantium_vs_Seljuk_c_1071_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> imperial authority weakened. The military aristocracy, enriched by earlier wars, sought to absorb the free peasantry, and officials curbed it by dismissing generals and cutting the thematic levies, leaving the empire dependent on unreliable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. John Skylitzes, Kekaumenos and Michael Attaleiates blamed Constantine IX for dismissing 50,000 standing troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> just as the Seljuk raids began. Rebellions such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-maniakes">George Maniakes</a>, Leo Tornikios and Isaac Komnenos drew the armies away from the frontiers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, who had overthrown the Ghaznavids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1055, first met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at the Battle of Kapetron on 18 September 1048.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> the Turks sacked Ani, Caesarea, Iconium and other cities, and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos IV Diogenes</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> on 26 August 1071. The victory brought few gains at first, but the civil wars that followed let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> and other Turkomans spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, often invited in as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> by rival claimants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>, emperor from 1081, lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and Smyrna by 1084, but in 1091 defeated a Seljuk and Pecheneg siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and in 1094 asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> for help, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a> in 1095.</p><p>The crusaders took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, which surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> on 19 June 1097, and John Doukas recovered Smyrna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, but the crusaders kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, and Iconium became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>. John II Komnenos retook the southern coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kastamonu">Kastamonu</a>, but not Iconium. Manuel I Komnenos made the sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> a client, until a dispute over Sebastea led to the ambush of his army of about 30,000 at Myriokephalon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The battle itself was tactically indecisive, and John Vatatzes later beat a Turkish army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Meander</a> valley, but no further Byzantine reconquest followed, and after Manuel's death in 1180 the Turks pressed further west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> collapsed in 1194, leaving only Rum, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> broke into the Anatolian beyliks by the 1260s. As the Turks gained ground, much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>'s population converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> activity. From the vacuum left by Rum rose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> would take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 29 May 1453.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Byzantine–Seljuk wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
